::ENG1_U02_L03_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.03] Passage: I promised I was not nervous, then dropped my note cards twice before saying hello. Question: Which point of view is used?{
=A. First person#Correct. The narrator uses I and my to describe the experience directly.
~B. Second person#Incorrect. The narrator does not address the reader as you.
~C. Third-person omniscient#Incorrect. The narrator does not describe many characters' thoughts.
~D. Third-person limited#Incorrect. The narrator uses I, not he or she.
}

::ENG1_U02_L03_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.03] Passage: The narrator knows that Maya is proud of her speech but that Jordan secretly hopes it ends quickly. Question: Which narrator perspective is used?{
~A. First person#Incorrect. The narrator does not use I to tell a personal experience.
=B. Third-person omniscient#Correct. The narrator knows private thoughts of more than one character.
~C. Second person#Incorrect. The passage does not speak directly to you.
~D. Dramatic dialogue#Incorrect. The passage describes thoughts rather than only spoken words.
}

::ENG1_U02_L03_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.03] Passage: The town celebrated "No Screen Day" by posting hourly updates online. Question: What creates irony?{
~A. The town has a celebration.#Incorrect. A celebration alone does not create irony.
~B. The updates happen hourly.#Incorrect. This detail matters, but the irony comes from using screens for No Screen Day.
=C. The town uses screens to promote a day without screens.#Correct. The action contradicts the purpose of the event.
~D. The day has a name.#Incorrect. The name itself is not enough without the contradiction.
}

::ENG1_U02_L03_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.03] Passage: The narrator calls the school rule "a masterpiece of fairness" after explaining that only students with last names A-M may use the library. Question: What is being satirized?{
~A. Students reading silently#Incorrect. The passage criticizes unfair rules, not reading.
~B. Alphabetical order in dictionaries#Incorrect. The alphabet is not the target of criticism.
~C. The size of the library#Incorrect. Library size is not mentioned.
=D. A rule that claims fairness while treating students unequally#Correct. The praise is sarcastic because the rule is not fair.
}

::ENG1_U02_L03_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.03] Passage: She smiled at the invitation, but the narrator tells us her hands shook under the table. Question: How does the narrator affect meaning?{
~A. The narrator proves she is angry at the table.#Incorrect. Shaking hands suggest hidden nervousness, not anger at furniture.
=B. The narrator reveals feelings that her outward smile hides.#Correct. The narrator gives readers information other characters may not notice.
~C. The narrator changes the invitation into a letter.#Incorrect. The invitation remains an invitation.
~D. The narrator removes all conflict from the scene.#Incorrect. The hidden nervousness creates tension.
}
